WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.454
Almost everyone has heard of order blocks. But most traders just mark any random candle – and then wonder why price cuts straight through it. The difference is in the details.

00:00:10.454 --> 00:00:19.000
In this video you'll learn how to find an order block, how to rate its quality, where a sensible entry is – and where your stop belongs.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:30.760
Important up front: this is educational content, not investment advice. Trading involves risk of loss. Practise new strategies on a demo account first.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:42.055
An order block is a price zone where large market participants have likely placed a lot of orders. On the chart, it's easy to spot.

00:00:42.055 --> 00:00:44.750
Gold falls over several candles.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:51.207
Then the market turns up with a powerful impulse. A move like that rarely happens by chance.

00:00:51.207 --> 00:00:59.858
The last red candle before this impulse is the bullish order block. This is where buying started – and there are often still orders waiting there.

00:00:59.858 --> 00:01:07.386
Later, price comes back into exactly this zone. That's called the test, or mitigation, of the order block.

00:01:07.386 --> 00:01:16.620
And here the market reacts higher again. A bearish order block is the mirror image: the last green candle before a strong sell-off.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:28.385
Not every red candle is an order block. For a zone to count, something important has to happen afterwards. Let's go through it step by step.

00:01:28.385 --> 00:01:34.737
The market is in a downtrend. Note the last lower high – that's the line that has to be broken later.

00:01:34.737 --> 00:01:39.705
Then comes the last red candle before the buyers take over. That's our candidate.

00:01:39.705 --> 00:01:49.140
Now the first condition: the impulse breaks above the last lower high. Only this break of structure turns the candle into a real order block.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:57.504
The second condition: the impulse is so fast that it leaves a Fair Value Gap behind. That shows how strong the buyers were.

00:01:57.504 --> 00:02:02.394
Now the zone is marked: from the low to the high of the last red candle.

00:02:02.394 --> 00:02:12.463
On the pullback, price tests the zone and turns higher again. Without the break of structure and without the gap, it would have been just an ordinary candle.

00:02:15.263 --> 00:02:22.686
There are plenty of order blocks. The question is: which ones are worth trading? For that we use five criteria.

00:02:22.686 --> 00:02:27.001
Here gold first forms a low. Stops are sitting below it.

00:02:27.001 --> 00:02:34.294
The last red candle wicks below this low and grabs the liquidity. An order block with a sweep is much stronger.

00:02:34.294 --> 00:02:39.079
Then the impulse breaks above the last high. A clear break of structure.

00:02:39.079 --> 00:02:42.741
And it leaves a Fair Value Gap right above the zone.

00:02:42.741 --> 00:02:52.019
The order block also sits below the fifty percent level of the whole move – in other words, in discount. For buys, that's the cheaper area.

00:02:52.019 --> 00:03:02.298
And finally: the zone is still untouched. An order block that has already been tested several times usually has few open orders left.

00:03:05.098 --> 00:03:12.443
You've found a good order block. Now it's about the entry. There are three common approaches – each with pros and cons.

00:03:12.443 --> 00:03:16.105
Here's our zone, formed before a powerful impulse.

00:03:16.105 --> 00:03:25.304
Price pulls back. Option one: a limit order at the top edge of the order block. You're very likely to get filled, but your stop is the furthest away.

00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:34.269
Option two: you wait for the middle of the zone, the fifty percent level. Your stop gets tighter, but sometimes the order doesn't get filled.

00:03:34.269 --> 00:03:39.472
In this example the wick reaches exactly the middle and the entry is triggered.

00:03:39.472 --> 00:03:51.728
Option three: you wait until price shows a reaction inside the zone – for example a Change of Character on the lower timeframe. It costs you a bit of price, but it filters out a lot of failed attempts.

00:03:51.728 --> 00:04:00.074
Which option fits depends on your style. What matters is that you decide in advance – not in the heat of the moment.

00:04:02.874 --> 00:04:09.200
The entry is only half the job. Just as important is where your stop sits and where your target is.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:12.966
Once again we have an order block after a strong impulse.

00:04:12.966 --> 00:04:16.811
Price comes back and the entry is taken inside the zone.

00:04:16.811 --> 00:04:26.142
The stop goes below the low of the order block, with a little buffer. Gold often prints long wicks – a stop right at the edge gets taken out quickly.

00:04:26.142 --> 00:04:35.655
A good target is the next pool of liquidity, here the old high. Before entering, check whether the distance to the target is actually worth it compared to the stop.

00:04:35.655 --> 00:04:49.982
In this example price runs above the high. But the same rule applies: if a candle closes clearly below the order block, the zone is invalid. Then the stop does its job – no discussion.

00:04:52.782 --> 00:04:54.328
So remember:

00:04:54.328 --> 00:04:58.329
An order block is valid as long as price reacts at the zone.

00:04:58.329 --> 00:05:03.644
If a candle closes clearly below it, it's invalid – and you're out.

00:05:06.444 --> 00:05:08.069
Let's sum up.

00:05:08.069 --> 00:05:12.802
A bullish order block is the last red candle before a strong impulse higher.

00:05:12.802 --> 00:05:17.717
It only counts if the impulse breaks structure and leaves a Fair Value Gap.

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:23.626
The strongest zones have taken liquidity first, sit in discount and are still untouched.

00:05:23.626 --> 00:05:28.777
Decide on your entry in advance: top edge, middle or confirmation.

00:05:28.777 --> 00:05:35.528
The stop goes below the zone. And risk only one to two percent of your account per trade.

00:05:35.528 --> 00:05:51.781
